course: dictionary
lesson: 5186
title: Roman numerals: IX
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Roman numerals use letters to stand for numbers. I is 1, V is 5 and X is 10. Once you know these three, you can read many more numbers. «On a Roman clock I = 1, V = 5 and X = 10, so III is 3, VI is 6, IX is 9 and XII is 12.»
explain: IX is made of two letters, I and X. When a smaller letter comes before a bigger one, you take it away: I before X means 10 minus 1, which is 9. Think of it as X with one taken off.
explain: When a smaller letter comes after a bigger one, you add it: VI is 5 plus 1, which is 6. So the rule depends on the order of the letters, and that is the clue to check every time.
fact: IX is the Roman numeral for 9.
fact: When a smaller numeral comes before a bigger one, you subtract: I before X is 10 minus 1, which is 9.
fact: IX is used in names like King Louis IX of France, where it is read as «the ninth».
fact: Chapters in books are sometimes numbered in Roman numerals, such as Chapter IX.
fact: Some clocks show 4 as IIII, because clockmakers liked how it looked.
exercise: «On a Roman clock IX is 9.» What number is IX? | 10 | 1 | 11 | *9
exercise: What is VI on a Roman clock? | 5 | 16 | *6 | 4
exercise: In «Chapter IX» of a book, which chapter is it? | *Chapter 9 | Chapter 10 | Chapter 19 | Chapter 1
test: What number is XII on a Roman clock? | 10 | 2 | 21 | *12
test: How do you read «Louis IX» aloud? | Louis the first | Louis ten | Louis one | *Louis the ninth
exam: What sum does IX show? | 10 plus 1 | 5 plus 5 | *10 minus 1 | 1 times 10
